A||||7||Princess46||It won't work!!!!!||||9||
Z||000000||Princess46||04-25-2000||10:27 PM||||My boyfriend has a fiero and is having problems...maybe you can help...<P>When he tried to start it is starts fine in neutral...but when he tries to shift into another gear it has a grinding sound.  Then if he tries to start the car in another gear it jerks forward and then stops. He said that the clutch is softer than usual.  He has also just bled the clutch yesterday because he cleaned it with soap (which was VERY stupid!).  I think that he might have gotten air into his clutch...what do you think?<P>He thinks that it might be the clutch or master cylinder...do you think that is possible!?!?  He knows NOTHING about cars!<P>If you can, please help.  It would be much appreciated!<BR>					Thanks,<BR>				Erika Ellsworth||||reg||9
Z||000001||stimpy||04-25-2000||10:40 PM||||Erika, may I make a suggestion? Using utmost care, remove and replace... YOUR BOYFRIEND!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> just kiddin.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Princess46||04-25-2000||10:47 PM||||lol...NOT!<P>I seriously NEED to know what to DO!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||04-25-2000||11:08 PM||||The hydraulic clutch system on a Fiero is sort of touchy and uses a special procedure to bleed (see below link). My next question is, why was he messing with it in the first place, There may be another problem other than air in the system, which there probably is.<P> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||87ECO5||04-26-2000||02:32 AM||||Batboy is probably right, Princess.  Air in the lines can wreak havoc on a Fiero's hydraulic system.<P>However, if you/he/whoever bleeds the system and still have problems, look at these possibilities:<P>- if it's an earlier model Fiero, it could have the aluminum clutch pedal, which can get bent and won't allow the clutch to fully disengage.<BR>- he didn't put ATF in the lines, did he!?  (sorry, Drew!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   )<BR>- could be time for a new clutch<P>I doubt it's the master cylinder.  Generally, when the master cylinder goes, it'll leak fluid.<P>Hope this helps ...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Homicidal||04-26-2000||02:49 AM||||Isn't it cute when your girlfriend posts something and REALLY makes you feel dumb?<P>Yes people, I and the dumb boyfriend who knows nothing about cars is actually the same person!<P>How do I go about checking the bent pedal?  ||||reg||10
Z||000006||mwbackus||04-26-2000||04:58 AM||||Place a magnet on the clutch pedal and if it does not stick, replace the pedal.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>Its best not to even mess with the aluminum ones. <BR>After that and a good proper bleeding, I would check the travel on the clutch shaft lever and ensure that it has not cracked where it clamps to clutch shaft.<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||04-26-2000||09:30 AM||||You could always swap trannys for an auto.||||reg||1
